{"name":"Frenzy Plant","hex":"#9cc780","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"frenzy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of wild activity or panic.","A violent agitation of the mind approaching madness; rage."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Mad; frantic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/frenzy"},{"term":"plant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An organism that is not an animal, especially an organism capable of photosynthesis, and typically a small or herbaceous organism of this kind, rather than a tree.","An organism of the kingdom Plantae, and now specifically, a living organism of the Embryophyta (land plants) or of the Chlorophyta (green algae), a eukaryote that includes double-membraned chloroplasts in its cells containing chlorophyll a and b, or any organism closely related to such an organism."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To place (a seed or plant) in soil or other substrate in order that it may live and grow.","To furnish or supply with plants."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plant"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frenzy","blocks":[{"text":"Frenzy is a 1972 British neo-noir thriller film directed and produced by Alfred Hitchcock. It is the penultimate feature film of his extensive career. The screenplay by Anthony Shaffer is based on the 1966 novel Goodbye Piccadilly, Farewell Leicester Square by Arthur La Bern. The film stars Jon Finch, Alec McCowen and Barry Foster and features Billie Whitelaw, Anna Massey, Barbara Leigh-Hunt, Bernard Cribbins and Vivien Merchant. The original music score was composed by Ron Goodwin."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/eb/Frenzy_movieposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frenzy"},{"title":"Plant","blocks":[{"text":"Plants are the eukaryotic organisms that constitute the kingdom Plantae. They are predominantly photosynthetic or came from photosynthetic ancestors, meaning that they obtain their energy from sunlight by using the green pigment chlorophyll in their chloroplasts to produce sugars from carbon dioxide and water. Over the course of time, some plants have evolved to be parasitic, meaning they take energy from other organisms. Except for glaucophytes, some green algae and a few red algae, most plants are multicellular."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Fr%C3%BChling_bl%C3%BChender_Kirschenbaum.jpg/330px-Fr%C3%BChling_bl%C3%BChender_Kirschenbaum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plant"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Frenzy_Henieretta_Street.JPG/500px-Frenzy_Henieretta_Street.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Frenzy_Henieretta_Street.JPG/1280px-Frenzy_Henieretta_Street.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Frenzy_Henieretta_Street.JPG/500px-Frenzy_Henieretta_Street.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"3 Henrietta Street in Covent Garden was the flat of the \"Necktie Murderer\", Robert Rusk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrenzy_Henieretta_Street.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Micrasterias_radiata.jpg/500px-Micrasterias_radiata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Micrasterias_radiata.jpg/500px-Micrasterias_radiata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMicrasterias_radiata.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/t